  "fields": {
    "title": "Skeuomorphism",
    "textContent": {
      "nodeType": "document",
      "data": {},
      "content": [
        {
          "nodeType": "heading-3",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Description",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Skeuomorphism refers to a design style that imitates another material or the form of an older, analogue and already familiar object, although the design has no concrete connection to the function in most cases. The visual reference to an already familiar object is intended to promote a more intuitive approach to new interfaces. One example of this style is the interface design of early Apple IOS systems (up to version 6), which used a lot of Skeuomorphism, for example in the notes or calculator app. An other example are early e-book readers that tried to imitate the analogue turning of a page through animation. ",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "embedded-asset-block",
          "data": {
            "target": {
              "sys": {
                "id": "2ptFRbXnDoJ8YALGlGwwSN",
